        <section class="my-3" aria-labelledby="transaction-decoder-faq">
    <h2 id="transaction-decoder-faq">Transaction Decoder FAQ</h2>

    <div class="accordion" id="transactionDecoderFaq">
    <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#transactionDecoderFaq3d01b10152" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="transactionDecoderFaq3d01b10152">
            What does the Bitcoin Transaction Decoder extract?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="transactionDecoderFaq3d01b10152" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#transactionDecoderFaq">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p>For each supported legacy P2PKH input, the decoder displays:</p>

            <ul class="mb-0">
                <li>The DER-encoded ECDSA signature.</li>
                <li>The signature's <strong>R, S, and Z values</strong>.</li>
                <li>The public key and public-key hash.</li>
                <li>A warning when the same R-value appears more than once.</li>
                <li>The recovered private key and signature nonce when duplicate R-values make recovery possible.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#transactionDecoderFaq03e267820b" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="transactionDecoderFaq03e267820b">
            What is ECDSA?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="transactionDecoderFaq03e267820b" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#transactionDecoderFaq">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p>The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_Curve_Digital_Signature_Algorithm"
                      title="Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm"><strong>Elliptic Curve Digital Signature
                        Algorithm (ECDSA)</strong></a> is a cryptographic algorithm used to create and verify digital
                signatures. Bitcoin uses ECDSA with the <strong>secp256k1 elliptic curve</strong> to prove that the
                owner of a private key authorized a transaction without revealing that private key.</p>

            <p class="mb-0">A wallet signs a transaction hash with its private key, producing an ECDSA signature
                containing two integers: <strong>R</strong> and <strong>S</strong>. Bitcoin nodes verify the signature
                with the corresponding public key. <strong>Z</strong> is the integer representation of the transaction
                hash being signed.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#transactionDecoderFaq4681d89d1c" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="transactionDecoderFaq4681d89d1c">
            What is DER encoding?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="transactionDecoderFaq4681d89d1c" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#transactionDecoderFaq">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p><strong>Distinguished Encoding Rules (DER)</strong> is a strict binary format for encoding data
                structures defined with Abstract Syntax Notation One (ASN.1). In Bitcoin, DER encodes an
                <strong>ECDSA signature</strong> as a sequence containing the two signature integers,
                <strong>R</strong> and <strong>S</strong>, so nodes can parse and validate them consistently.</p>

            <p class="mb-0">A Bitcoin transaction signature normally has a one-byte signature hash type, such as
                <code>0x01</code> for <strong>SIGHASH_ALL</strong>, appended after the DER-encoded signature. This byte
                is not part of DER; it specifies which parts of the transaction the signature commits to.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#transactionDecoderFaq1aefeba65b" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="transactionDecoderFaq1aefeba65b">
            What is the ECDSA weak-signature vulnerability?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="transactionDecoderFaq1aefeba65b" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#transactionDecoderFaq">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p>Each <strong>ECDSA signature</strong> requires a secret per-signature nonce. The nonce must be unique and
                unpredictable. Reusing the same nonce for two different transaction hashes produces the same R-value
                and can expose both the nonce and the private key:</p>

            <pre class="code-block"><button class="copy" title="Copy" data-bs-toggle="tooltip"><i class="far fa-copy"></i></button><code>private key = (z1 * s2 - z2 * s1) / (r * (s1 - s2)) mod n</code></pre>

            <p>A correctly generated signature does not reveal its private key. This decoder checks for duplicate
                R-values among supported inputs in the submitted transaction and attempts recovery only when the
                required signature data is available.</p>

            <p class="mb-0">Weak random-number generation has caused real-world Bitcoin wallet vulnerabilities. For
                example, a 2013 <a href="https://bitcoin.org/en/alert/2013-08-11-android">Android security
                    vulnerability</a> required affected wallets to generate new keys and move their funds.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
    <h2 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button  collapsed " type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#transactionDecoderFaq708348c302" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="transactionDecoderFaq708348c302">
            Which Bitcoin transaction input types are supported?
        </button>
    </h2>
    <div id="transactionDecoderFaq708348c302" class="accordion-collapse collapse " data-bs-parent="#transactionDecoderFaq">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            <p class="mb-0">The decoder currently supports legacy <strong>Pay-to-Public-Key-Hash (P2PKH)</strong>
                inputs containing an ECDSA signature and public key in the input script. Coinbase, SegWit, Taproot, and
                other non-P2PKH inputs use different script or witness structures and are not currently supported.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
